Sure! I'll provide you with an example of what you might do to help get rid of boredom:

$$\frac{0.5 \, \text{m}^2 = 1 \, \text{ft}^2}$$

It's common in many engineering applications to convert between metric and U.S. customary units, and knowing conversion factors between them is useful in these cases. When working with both metric and U.S. customary units in the same problem, the conversion factors are almost always given, not in terms of kilometers or miles. In these problems, you often round numbers to match U.S. standards since most calculations will be done in metric units but the final result is converted to standard units (e.g., 1 foot equivalent of 0.62 mile). For instance, in the United States, people are more familiar with Fahrenheit temperature scales rather than Celsius, and the formula would look like this: $$F = \frac{9}{5}
